1. What is Continuous Integration (CI)?

Continuous Integration (CI) is a software development practice where developers frequently merge their code changes into a shared repository. Every change is automatically tested and verified.

Benefits
Detect bugs early
Improve code quality
Automate testing
Faster development
Reduce manual work

Example:

Developer pushes code to GitHub.
GitHub Actions automatically runs tests.
If tests pass, code is approved.
If tests fail, developers are notified.
2. GitHub Actions

GitHub Actions is GitHub's automation platform that allows you to build, test, and deploy applications directly from your GitHub repository.

Common Uses:

Run tests
Build projects
Deploy websites
Build Docker images
Send notifications
3. Workflow Automation

A workflow is an automated process defined in a YAML file.

Example:

name: Build Project

on: push

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Run command
        run: echo "Hello World"

When code is pushed, GitHub automatically runs this workflow.

4. Events and Triggers

Workflows start when specific events occur.

Push Event

Triggered when code is pushed.

on:
  push:
Pull Request Event

Triggered when a pull request is opened or updated.

on:
  pull_request:
Schedule Event

Runs automatically at specified times.

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 0 * * *'

Runs every day at midnight.

Manual Workflow

Triggered manually from GitHub.

on:
  workflow_dispatch:
5. Workflow Directory Structure

GitHub Actions workflows are stored inside:

project/
│
├── .github/
│   └── workflows/
│       └── ci.yml
│
└── source code

GitHub automatically detects workflow files inside .github/workflows.

6. Key Components of GitHub Actions
Workflow

A complete automation process.

name: CI Pipeline
Job

A workflow contains one or more jobs.

jobs:
  build:

Example:

jobs:
  build:
  test:

Build and test are separate jobs.

Step

A job consists of steps.

steps:
  - name: Install Dependencies
    run: npm install
Action

Reusable automation units.

Example:

uses: actions/checkout@v4

This action checks out repository code.

Runner

A machine that executes workflow jobs.

Example:

runs-on: ubuntu-latest

GitHub provides the machine.

7. Jobs and Matrix Strategies

Matrix strategy allows testing on multiple configurations.

Example:

strategy:
  matrix:
    os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest]

GitHub runs jobs on:

Ubuntu
Windows

Example:

jobs:
  test:
    strategy:
      matrix:
        python-version: [3.9, 3.10, 3.11]

Tests run for all Python versions.

Benefits:

Cross-platform testing
Multiple language versions
Better compatibility
8. Steps and Shell Commands

Commands can be executed directly.

steps:
  - name: List files
    run: ls

Multiple commands:

run: |
  npm install
  npm test

Windows example:

run: dir
